  "issue": {
    "body": "I don't think you can use F-Strings in assert statements.  I get \"invalid syntax\" if I try.\r\n\r\nThe line: \r\n`directory = os.path.normpath(os.path.join(spec.origin, \"..\", \"statics\"))`\r\nAlways assumes there is a folder `statics`?  I believe the standard is `static` and not `statics` and the user is not given the option to circumvent the step.  Does it make more sense to only include this step if `directory is None`.\r\n\r\nThe directory argument does not get the package path prepended, which I feel is implied in the documentation.\r\n\r\nThe `assert os.path.isdir(directory)` will fail every time since it will return the `__init__.py` of a package.",
    "closed_at": "2019-07-15T08:51:24Z",
    "created_at": "2019-06-06T15:04:51Z",
    "html_url": "https://github.com/Kludex/starlette/issues/534",
    "number": 534,
    "state": "closed",
    "title": "get_directories in staticfiles.py has bad asserts",
